-- Q1: medium / L3 — Transfer pricing arm's length standard application
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Harmon Industries, a U.S. corporation, sells inventory to its wholly-owned subsidiary in Country K at $50 per unit. Comparable unrelated-party sales of the same product in Country K average $80 per unit. The IRS invokes Section 482 and reallocates $1,200,000 in additional income to Harmon (40,000 units). Harmon did not maintain contemporaneous transfer pricing documentation. What penalty exposure does Harmon face on the $1,200,000 reallocation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A 20% penalty for substantial valuation misstatement if the transfer price is 200% or more of the correct price", "A 40% penalty for gross valuation misstatement if the transfer price is 400% or more of the correct price", "No penalty because Section 482 reallocations are automatic adjustments without penalty consequences", "A 10% penalty for negligent understatement of income from related-party transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 482 and Section 6662(e) impose a 20% penalty for substantial valuation misstatements when the transfer price deviates by 200% or more from the arm's length price. Harmon's $50 price versus the $80 arm's length price (62.5% of correct) triggers the substantial misstatement threshold, and the lack of contemporaneous documentation eliminates the reasonable cause defense. Wrong (B): The 40% gross valuation misstatement penalty requires a 400% or more deviation, which is not met here since $50 is 62.5% of $80, not 25% or less. Wrong (C): Section 482 reallocations do carry penalty exposure under Section 6662(e) when the taxpayer lacks adequate documentation. Wrong (D): There is no standalone 10% penalty for related-party transfer pricing misstatements; the applicable penalties are 20% or 40% under Section 6662.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Section 78 gross-up for deemed-paid FTCs
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Oakridge Corp, a U.S. C corporation, has a CFC that earns $1,000,000 of GILTI tested income and pays $150,000 in foreign income taxes. Oakridge claims deemed-paid foreign tax credits under Section 960 (after the 80% haircut, the creditable amount is $120,000). Under Section 78, how must Oakridge treat the deemed-paid taxes for U.S. tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deduct the $120,000 as a foreign tax expense against ordinary income", "Include $120,000 as a dividend (gross-up) in gross income in addition to the GILTI inclusion", "Exclude the $120,000 from both income and deductions because it is already reflected in the GILTI computation", "Reduce the GILTI inclusion by $120,000 to avoid double counting the foreign taxes"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 78 requires that deemed-paid foreign tax credits be included in the U.S. shareholder's gross income as a dividend. This gross-up ensures the credit offsets the additional income, maintaining parity with direct foreign tax credits. The $120,000 (after the 80% haircut) is added to Oakridge's income. Wrong (A): Deemed-paid credits are not deducted as expenses; they are claimed as credits, with the Section 78 gross-up ensuring income inclusion. Wrong (C): Section 78 explicitly requires inclusion in income; the taxes are not ignored. Wrong (D): The GILTI inclusion is not reduced by deemed-paid taxes; instead, the Section 78 gross-up increases income and the credit offsets the resulting tax.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — GILTI high-tax exclusion election
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Vantage Corp owns two CFCs. CFC-1 has $3,000,000 of tested income and pays $700,000 in foreign taxes (23.3% effective rate). CFC-2 has $2,000,000 of tested income and pays $200,000 in foreign taxes (10% effective rate). Vantage is evaluating whether to elect the GILTI high-tax exclusion under Treas. Reg. Section 1.951A-2(c)(7), which excludes CFC income taxed above 90% of the U.S. corporate rate (18.9%) from tested income. Assess the impact of this election.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The election excludes CFC-1 only, reducing aggregate tested income to $2,000,000 but also forfeiting CFC-1's $700,000 of foreign tax credits", "The election excludes CFC-2 only because its 10% rate exceeds the effective threshold after aggregation", "The election excludes both CFCs because both effective rates exceed the 18.9% threshold", "The election must be applied to all CFCs consistently, so Vantage cannot selectively exclude CFC-1 while retaining CFC-2 credits"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.951A-2(c)(7), the GILTI high-tax exclusion applies on a tested-unit basis. CFC-1's 23.3% effective rate exceeds 18.9% (90% of 21%), so its income is excluded from tested income. CFC-2 at 10% does not meet the threshold and remains in the GILTI computation. The tradeoff: aggregate tested income drops to $2,000,000 but CFC-1's $700,000 in foreign tax credits are forfeited. Wrong (B): CFC-2's 10% rate is below 18.9%, so it does not qualify for exclusion. Wrong (C): Only CFC-1 exceeds the 18.9% threshold; CFC-2 at 10% does not. Wrong (D): The election applies to all tested units meeting the threshold automatically — Vantage cannot pick and choose, but the election only excludes qualifying units, not all CFCs.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: hard / L4 — Branch vs subsidiary decision for foreign operations
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Fenwick Technologies plans to open operations in Country Z, which has a 30% corporate tax rate. The Country Z operations are expected to generate $500,000 in losses for the first three years, then $2,000,000 in annual profits. Fenwick's U.S. taxable income is $10,000,000. Its tax advisor is recommending whether to operate as a foreign branch or a foreign subsidiary (CFC). Recommend the optimal structure considering both the loss years and profitable years.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Operate as a subsidiary throughout because CFC losses reduce GILTI tested income immediately", "Operate as a branch initially to use losses against U.S. income, then incorporate as a subsidiary when profitable to access the Section 250 deduction", "Operate as a subsidiary initially because foreign losses create excess foreign tax credit carryforwards", "Operate as a branch throughout because the 30% Country Z rate always generates excess credits that offset U.S. tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): A foreign branch allows Fenwick to deduct losses directly against U.S. income during the loss years, providing immediate tax benefit. Once profitable, converting to a CFC subsidiary allows the Section 250 deduction on GILTI (reducing the effective U.S. rate) and deferral benefits. This branch-then-incorporate strategy optimizes the tax treatment across both phases. Wrong (A): CFC losses are tested losses that offset other CFCs' tested income but cannot directly reduce Fenwick's U.S. taxable income; the losses would be trapped. Wrong (C): A subsidiary does not generate foreign tax credit carryforwards from losses — there are no foreign taxes paid when the operation is losing money. Wrong (D): Operating as a branch when profitable means the 30% Country Z rate creates excess credits (30% vs. 21% U.S. rate) with limited carryforward utility, which is less efficient than the subsidiary structure with the Section 250 deduction.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q5: easy / L2 — FTC credit vs deduction election
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Westfield Inc., a U.S. corporation, pays $50,000 in foreign taxes on $300,000 of foreign-source income. Its worldwide taxable income is $1,500,000 with U.S. tax liability of $315,000. The FTC limitation is $42,000. How should Westfield classify the $8,000 difference between taxes paid and the limitation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Excess foreign tax credit of $8,000, carried back one year and forward ten years", "A permanent loss that cannot be recovered in any future period", "An additional deduction against current-year taxable income", "A refundable credit payable by the IRS in the current year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 904(c), when foreign taxes paid ($50,000) exceed the FTC limitation ($42,000), the $8,000 excess may be carried back one year and forward ten years within the same basket. This prevents the permanent loss of credits due to timing differences. Wrong (B): Excess credits are not permanently lost; the carryback and carryforward provisions specifically preserve them. Wrong (C): Once the credit election is made for the year, the excess cannot be converted to a deduction; it remains a credit carryover. Wrong (D): Foreign tax credits are nonrefundable — they can only offset U.S. tax liability, not generate a refund.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Section 250 taxable income limitation on FDII + GILTI deductions
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Pinnacle Corp has GILTI of $4,000,000 and FDII of $3,000,000. Its taxable income before the Section 250 deduction is $5,000,000. The combined Section 250 deduction would be $2,000,000 (50% of GILTI) + $1,125,000 (37.5% of FDII) = $3,125,000, which exceeds taxable income. Evaluate how the taxable income limitation under Section 250(a)(2) affects Pinnacle's deduction.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The deduction is limited to $5,000,000 and both components are reduced proportionally", "The GILTI deduction takes priority and is claimed in full; only the FDII deduction is reduced", "The combined deduction of $3,125,000 is allowed in full because it does not exceed taxable income", "The excess deduction of $0 creates a net operating loss that carries forward under Section 172"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 250(a)(2) limits the combined GILTI and FDII deductions to taxable income. Here, the combined deduction of $3,125,000 does not exceed the $5,000,000 taxable income, so no limitation applies and Pinnacle claims the full $3,125,000. The limitation only triggers when the combined deduction would exceed taxable income. Wrong (A): Proportional reduction only applies when the combined deduction exceeds taxable income, which is not the case here ($3,125,000 < $5,000,000). Wrong (B): There is no priority ordering; both components are reduced ratably if the limitation applies. Wrong (D): The Section 250 deduction cannot create a net operating loss; the taxable income limitation prevents this.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: easy / L2 — PFIC qualified electing fund (QEF) annual inclusion
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Grayson, a U.S. individual, owns shares in a foreign mutual fund that qualifies as a PFIC. Grayson makes a QEF election under Section 1295. The fund reports $30,000 of ordinary earnings and $10,000 of net capital gain for the year but distributes nothing to Grayson. What must Grayson report on his U.S. return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Nothing, because no distribution was received during the year", "Only the $30,000 of ordinary earnings, because capital gains are deferred until distribution", "$40,000 total — $30,000 as ordinary income and $10,000 as long-term capital gain", "$40,000 as ordinary income because all PFIC inclusions are taxed at ordinary rates"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 1293, a QEF election requires the U.S. shareholder to include their pro rata share of both ordinary earnings ($30,000) and net capital gain ($10,000) annually, regardless of distributions. The capital gain retains its character as long-term capital gain. Wrong (A): The QEF election specifically overrides the distribution requirement — income is included currently whether or not distributed. Wrong (B): Both ordinary earnings and net capital gain must be included annually under Section 1293(a); capital gains are not deferred. Wrong (D): Net capital gain retains its character as long-term capital gain under the QEF rules; it is not recharacterized as ordinary income.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — De minimis rule for Subpart F income
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Corbin LLC owns 100% of a CFC with gross income of $15,000,000. Of this, $700,000 is foreign personal holding company income (FPHCI) that would otherwise be Subpart F income. The remaining $14,300,000 is active manufacturing income. Under the Subpart F de minimis rule, how is the $700,000 of FPHCI treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Excluded from Subpart F because it is less than 5% of gross income and less than $1,000,000", "Excluded from Subpart F because it is less than 5% of gross income only", "Included in Subpart F income because it exceeds the $500,000 de minimis threshold", "Included in Subpart F income, but only the amount exceeding $750,000 is taxable"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 954(b)(3)(A), Subpart F income is excluded if it is both less than the lesser of $1,000,000 or 5% of the CFC's gross income. Here, 5% of $15,000,000 is $750,000, and $700,000 is below both the $750,000 (5%) threshold and the $1,000,000 cap. Both conditions are satisfied, so the de minimis rule excludes the $700,000 from Subpart F. Wrong (B): The de minimis rule requires meeting both thresholds (under $1,000,000 and under 5%), not just the 5% test alone. Wrong (C): There is no $500,000 de minimis threshold; the thresholds are $1,000,000 and 5% of gross income. Wrong (D): There is no partial inclusion mechanism under the de minimis rule; it is all-or-nothing.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Previously taxed earnings (PTEP) under Section 959
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Dalton Corp owns 100% of a CFC that had $800,000 of Subpart F income included in Dalton's gross income in Year 1. In Year 2, the CFC distributes $500,000 to Dalton from those previously taxed earnings. How should Dalton treat the $500,000 distribution under Section 959?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Exclude the $500,000 from gross income because it was previously taxed under Subpart F", "Include the $500,000 as a dividend eligible for the Section 245A participation exemption", "Include the $500,000 as ordinary dividend income subject to the normal corporate tax rate", "Exclude only $400,000 (50% of the original Subpart F inclusion) and include $100,000 as a dividend"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 959(a) provides that distributions of previously taxed earnings and profits (PTEP) are excluded from the U.S. shareholder's gross income to avoid double taxation. Since Dalton already included $800,000 in Year 1, the $500,000 distribution from those earnings is tax-free. Wrong (B): Section 245A does not apply to PTEP distributions; 245A applies to non-previously-taxed dividends from specified 10%-owned foreign corporations. Wrong (C): PTEP distributions are specifically excluded from income under Section 959 to prevent double taxation. Wrong (D): The full $500,000 is excluded — there is no 50% limitation on PTEP distributions.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Check-the-box election for foreign entities
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Helix Corp, a U.S. parent, forms a new foreign entity in Country M. The entity has two owners: Helix (90%) and an unrelated foreign investor (10%). Under the check-the-box regulations (Treas. Reg. Section 301.7701-3), the entity defaults to a foreign partnership classification. Helix files Form 8832 to elect corporate classification. What is the primary U.S. tax consequence of this election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The entity becomes a disregarded entity for U.S. tax purposes, and all income flows directly to Helix", "The entity is treated as a foreign corporation, potentially qualifying as a CFC subject to Subpart F and GILTI rules", "The election has no effect because multi-owner foreign entities are always classified as partnerships", "The entity is treated as a domestic corporation subject to full U.S. taxation on worldwide income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Treas. Reg. Section 301.7701-3(a), an eligible entity with two or more owners may elect corporate classification via Form 8832. Once classified as a corporation, it becomes a foreign corporation. With Helix owning 90%, the entity qualifies as a CFC under Section 957, subjecting its income to Subpart F and GILTI analysis. Wrong (A): A disregarded entity classification is only available for single-owner entities; this entity has two owners. Wrong (C): Multi-owner foreign entities may elect corporate classification under the check-the-box rules; the partnership default is not mandatory. Wrong (D): A check-the-box election to corporate status creates a foreign corporation, not a domestic one; only entities organized under U.S. law are domestic.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Tested loss CFC impact on aggregate GILTI
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Nexus Corp owns three CFCs. CFC-A has tested income of $4,000,000 and QBAI of $5,000,000. CFC-B has a tested loss of $1,500,000 and QBAI of $3,000,000. CFC-C has tested income of $2,000,000 and QBAI of $6,000,000. Calculate Nexus Corp's GILTI inclusion, noting that tested loss CFCs do not contribute QBAI to the aggregate calculation.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$4,500,000 GILTI inclusion using all three CFCs' QBAI", "Net CFC tested income of $4,500,000 minus DTIR of $1,100,000, for GILTI of $3,400,000", "Net CFC tested income of $4,500,000 minus DTIR of $1,400,000, for GILTI of $3,100,000", "Net CFC tested income of $5,500,000 minus DTIR of $800,000, for GILTI of $4,700,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 951A, net CFC tested income is $4,000,000 + $2,000,000 - $1,500,000 = $4,500,000. CFC-B's $3,000,000 QBAI is excluded because tested loss CFCs do not contribute QBAI. Aggregate QBAI is $5,000,000 + $6,000,000 = $11,000,000. DTIR = 10% x $11,000,000 = $1,100,000. GILTI = $4,500,000 - $1,100,000 = $3,400,000. Wrong (A): This ignores the DTIR offset entirely. Wrong (C): This incorrectly includes CFC-B's QBAI ($14,000,000 x 10% = $1,400,000), but tested loss CFCs are excluded from aggregate QBAI. Wrong (D): This fails to subtract CFC-B's tested loss from aggregate tested income and uses incorrect QBAI.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Form 5472 reporting for foreign-owned U.S. corporations
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Alpine USA Inc. is a domestic corporation that is 100% owned by a foreign parent company in Country J. During the year, Alpine pays $2,000,000 in royalties to its foreign parent. Alpine's tax advisor must determine the correct information return for reporting this related-party transaction. Which form must Alpine file?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Form 5471, Information Return of U.S. Persons With Respect to Certain Foreign Corporations", "Form 8865, Return of U.S. Persons With Respect to Certain Foreign Partnerships", "Form 926, Return by a U.S. Transferor of Property to a Foreign Corporation", "Form 5472, Information Return of a 25% Foreign-Owned U.S. Corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Form 5472 is required for any U.S. corporation that is at least 25% foreign-owned and has reportable transactions with related foreign parties. Alpine is 100% foreign-owned and pays royalties to its parent, triggering the Form 5472 requirement under Section 6038A. Wrong (A): Form 5471 is filed by U.S. persons who are shareholders of foreign corporations, not by foreign-owned U.S. corporations reporting inbound transactions. Wrong (B): Form 8865 reports interests in foreign partnerships and is irrelevant to Alpine's corporate structure. Wrong (C): Form 926 reports transfers of property to foreign corporations, not ongoing related-party payments.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Permanent establishment concept
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under most U.S. income tax treaties, what is the significance of a "permanent establishment" (PE)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A PE creates taxing jurisdiction, allowing the source country to tax the business profits attributable to it", "A PE exempts a foreign company from all source-country taxation regardless of its activities", "A PE requires the foreign company to incorporate a subsidiary in the source country", "A PE only applies to individuals, not to corporations conducting business in a foreign country"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the permanent establishment concept in U.S. tax treaties, a PE is a fixed place of business that creates taxing jurisdiction in the source country. Without a PE, the treaty generally prevents the source country from taxing the foreign enterprise's business profits. Wrong (B): A PE has the opposite effect — it enables source-country taxation, not exemption from it. Wrong (C): A PE does not require incorporation; it can be an office, branch, factory, or other fixed place of business. Wrong (D): The PE concept applies to all enterprises, including corporations, not just individuals.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Dual-consolidated loss rules
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Meridian Corp, a U.S. parent, operates a branch in Country P that generates a $2,000,000 loss. Country P allows Meridian's foreign affiliate to use the same loss to offset Country P income. Meridian wants to also use the $2,000,000 loss to offset its U.S. consolidated group income. Under the dual-consolidated loss rules of Section 1503(d), evaluate whether Meridian can claim the loss in both jurisdictions.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because branch losses are always deductible against U.S. income regardless of foreign use", "Yes, but only if the U.S. loss is limited to 50% of the total amount used in Country P", "No, because a dual-consolidated loss cannot offset income of the U.S. consolidated group if it also offsets income in a foreign jurisdiction, unless a domestic use agreement is filed", "No, because branch losses are never deductible for U.S. tax purposes under any circumstances"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 1503(d) prohibits a dual-consolidated loss from offsetting U.S. consolidated group income if the loss also offsets income in a foreign jurisdiction. However, the taxpayer may file a domestic use agreement certifying the loss will not be used abroad. If the loss is later used abroad, recapture is required. Wrong (A): The dual-consolidated loss rules specifically prevent double use of the same loss in both the U.S. and a foreign jurisdiction. Wrong (B): There is no 50% partial allowance mechanism; the rules are all-or-nothing unless a domestic use agreement is filed. Wrong (D): Branch losses are generally deductible for U.S. tax purposes; the restriction applies only when the same loss is also used in a foreign jurisdiction.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Subpart F full inclusion rule (70% threshold)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Calder Corp owns 100% of a CFC with gross income of $10,000,000. The CFC has $7,500,000 of foreign personal holding company income and $2,500,000 of active manufacturing income. Calder's tax advisor is analyzing how the Subpart F full inclusion rule under Section 954(b)(3)(B) applies. What is the Subpart F inclusion amount?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$7,500,000, because only the actual Subpart F income categories are included", "The full $10,000,000 of gross income is treated as Subpart F income under the full inclusion rule", "$2,500,000, because the active manufacturing income is reclassified as Subpart F when the threshold is exceeded", "$750,000, because only 10% of the FPHCI qualifies after applying the de minimis rule"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 954(b)(3)(B), if Subpart F income exceeds 70% of the CFC's total gross income, then the entire gross income is treated as Subpart F income. Here, $7,500,000 is 75% of $10,000,000, exceeding the 70% threshold, so all $10,000,000 is Subpart F income. Wrong (A): The full inclusion rule overrides the normal category-by-category approach when the 70% threshold is breached. Wrong (C): The active income is not separately reclassified — the entire gross income becomes Subpart F under the full inclusion rule. Wrong (D): The de minimis rule is the opposite end of the spectrum (under 5% and $1,000,000); it does not apply here.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — FBAR reporting threshold
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$What is the filing threshold for FinCEN Form 114 (FBAR), which requires U.S. persons to report foreign financial accounts?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Aggregate value of foreign accounts exceeds $50,000 at any time during the year", "Aggregate value of foreign accounts exceeds $25,000 at any time during the year", "Aggregate value of foreign accounts exceeds $100,000 at any time during the year", "Aggregate value of foreign accounts exceeds $10,000 at any time during the calendar year"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under 31 U.S.C. Section 5314 and implementing regulations, a U.S. person must file FinCEN Form 114 (FBAR) if the aggregate value of all foreign financial accounts exceeds $10,000 at any time during the calendar year. Wrong (A): The $50,000 threshold applies to FATCA reporting on Form 8938, not to the FBAR. Wrong (B): There is no $25,000 threshold for any foreign account reporting requirement. Wrong (C): The $100,000 threshold is not used for FBAR filing; the correct threshold is $10,000.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Section 956 investment in US property (loan exception)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Trellis Corp's CFC lends $5,000,000 to an unrelated U.S. borrower for 90 days and separately guarantees a $3,000,000 bank loan made to Trellis Corp (its U.S. parent). Under Section 956, which transaction constitutes an investment in U.S. property that triggers an income inclusion for Trellis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $5,000,000 loan to the unrelated U.S. borrower, because any loan to a U.S. person is U.S. property", "Only the $3,000,000 guarantee, because it is considered an obligation of a U.S. shareholder under Section 956", "Both transactions constitute U.S. property investments totaling $8,000,000", "Neither transaction, because loans and guarantees are excluded from the definition of U.S. property"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 956(c)(1)(C), U.S. property includes obligations of U.S. persons who are shareholders of the CFC. A CFC's guarantee of its U.S. parent's debt is treated as holding an obligation of the U.S. parent under Section 956(d). The $5,000,000 loan to an unrelated U.S. person does not trigger Section 956 because the borrower is not a U.S. shareholder. Wrong (A): Loans to unrelated U.S. persons are not U.S. property under Section 956; the rule targets obligations of U.S. shareholders and related parties. Wrong (C): Only the guarantee triggers Section 956; the unrelated-party loan does not. Wrong (D): Guarantees of shareholder obligations are specifically included in the definition of U.S. property under Section 956(d).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Treaty limitation on benefits (LOB) clause
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$FrancoChem SA, a Country F corporation, claims reduced withholding on royalties paid by its U.S. licensee under the U.S.-Country F tax treaty. The IRS challenges the claim, asserting that FrancoChem is a shell company owned by third-country residents and does not satisfy the treaty's limitation on benefits (LOB) article. What is the purpose of the LOB clause?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To increase withholding rates on all cross-border payments to foreign corporations", "To prevent treaty shopping by ensuring only bona fide residents of the treaty partner country receive treaty benefits", "To eliminate all withholding on royalty payments between treaty countries", "To require foreign corporations to pay U.S. corporate tax on their worldwide income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): The LOB clause in U.S. tax treaties prevents treaty shopping — the practice of routing income through entities in treaty countries to claim reduced rates when the beneficial owners reside in non-treaty jurisdictions. FrancoChem must demonstrate it is a qualified resident, such as meeting ownership, base erosion, or active trade or business tests. Wrong (A): LOB clauses do not increase rates; they restrict who may claim reduced rates. Wrong (C): LOB does not eliminate withholding — it ensures only qualifying residents receive reduced rates. Wrong (D): LOB provisions do not impose U.S. corporate tax on worldwide income of foreign corporations.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Income sourcing rules (Section 861-865)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Redmond Software Inc., a U.S. corporation, licenses its proprietary software to customers in Country G, generating $6,000,000 in royalties. Under the income sourcing rules of Sections 861-862, how is this royalty income sourced for purposes of computing the foreign tax credit limitation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["U.S.-source income because Redmond is a domestic corporation", "Foreign-source income because the royalties are paid for the use of property in Country G", "Split 50/50 between U.S. and foreign sources under the allocation rules", "The source depends entirely on where the software was developed"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 862(a)(4), royalties for the use of intangible property outside the United States are foreign-source income. Since the software is licensed for use in Country G, the $6,000,000 in royalties is foreign-source income. This increases Redmond's FTC limitation by expanding the foreign-source income numerator. Wrong (A): Income source for royalties is determined by where the intangible is used, not the residence of the licensor. Wrong (C): There is no automatic 50/50 allocation for royalties; source follows the place of use of the intangible. Wrong (D): The development location does not determine source for royalties; Section 862(a)(4) looks to the place of use or privilege.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — BEAT base erosion payment definition
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under Section 59A, which of the following is classified as a "base erosion payment" for purposes of the BEAT?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Dividends paid by a U.S. corporation to its domestic shareholders", "Salary payments to U.S. employees working domestically", "Cost of goods sold for inventory purchased from an unrelated foreign supplier", "Deductible royalty payments made to a related foreign party"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 59A(d), base erosion payments include deductible amounts paid or accrued to related foreign persons, such as royalties, interest, services fees, and depreciation on acquired assets. Royalty payments to a related foreign party are a classic base erosion payment. Wrong (A): Dividends to domestic shareholders are not deductible payments to foreign related parties. Wrong (B): Salary payments to U.S. employees are not payments to foreign related parties. Wrong (C): Cost of goods sold is generally excluded from the BEAT computation under Section 59A(d)(5), and payments to unrelated parties are not base erosion payments.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Section 367(a) recognition on outbound transfers
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Whitmore Corp, a U.S. corporation, transfers equipment with a fair market value of $2,000,000 and an adjusted basis of $800,000 to its newly formed CFC in exchange for stock. Under Section 367(a), what gain must Whitmore recognize on this transfer?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$1,200,000, because Section 367(a) overrides the nonrecognition rules of Section 351 for outbound transfers to foreign corporations", "$0, because Section 351 applies to all corporate formations regardless of whether the transferee is foreign", "$2,000,000, because the full fair market value is treated as a deemed sale", "$400,000, because only 50% of the built-in gain is recognized on outbound transfers"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 367(a) provides that a U.S. person transferring appreciated property to a foreign corporation in a Section 351 exchange must recognize gain as if the property were sold at fair market value. The gain is $2,000,000 - $800,000 = $1,200,000. This prevents taxpayers from stripping appreciated assets out of the U.S. tax base. Wrong (B): Section 367(a) specifically overrides Section 351 nonrecognition for transfers to foreign corporations. Wrong (C): The recognized amount is the gain ($1,200,000), not the full FMV. Wrong (D): There is no 50% recognition rule; the full built-in gain is recognized.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — GILTI specified interest expense offset
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Quorum Corp's CFC has tested income of $6,000,000, QBAI of $30,000,000, and specified interest expense of $400,000. Calculate Quorum's net GILTI inclusion, showing the impact of the specified interest expense on the deemed tangible income return (DTIR).$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$6,000,000 because specified interest expense does not affect the GILTI computation", "Net CFC tested income of $6,000,000 minus net DTIR of $2,600,000, for GILTI of $3,400,000", "Net CFC tested income of $6,000,000 minus DTIR of $3,000,000, for GILTI of $3,000,000", "Net CFC tested income of $5,600,000 minus DTIR of $3,000,000, for GILTI of $2,600,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 951A(b)(2), GILTI = net CFC tested income minus net DTIR. DTIR = 10% x $30,000,000 = $3,000,000. Net DTIR = $3,000,000 - $400,000 specified interest expense = $2,600,000. GILTI = $6,000,000 - $2,600,000 = $3,400,000. The specified interest expense reduces the DTIR, increasing the GILTI inclusion. Wrong (A): Specified interest expense directly reduces the DTIR under Section 951A(b)(2)(B). Wrong (C): This ignores the $400,000 specified interest expense offset against the DTIR. Wrong (D): Specified interest expense reduces DTIR, not tested income; tested income remains $6,000,000.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Section 960 deemed-paid credit 80% haircut mechanics
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Bryson Corp's CFC has $2,000,000 of GILTI tested income and pays $300,000 in foreign taxes. Bryson claims the Section 250 deduction (50%) and deemed-paid foreign tax credits under Section 960. Under Section 960(d)(1), the deemed-paid credit for GILTI is limited to 80% of the applicable foreign taxes. What is the deemed-paid credit amount and the Section 78 gross-up that Bryson must include?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deemed-paid credit of $300,000 with no gross-up required for GILTI inclusions", "Deemed-paid credit of $240,000 with no Section 78 gross-up because GILTI is already included in income", "Deemed-paid credit of $150,000 (50% haircut matching the Section 250 deduction rate) with a $150,000 gross-up", "Deemed-paid credit of $240,000 (80% of $300,000) with a Section 78 gross-up of $240,000 included in gross income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 960(d)(1), the deemed-paid credit for GILTI is limited to 80% of foreign taxes, giving $300,000 x 80% = $240,000. Section 78 requires this $240,000 to be grossed up into Bryson's income as a dividend, increasing the total GILTI-related income before the Section 250 deduction is applied. Wrong (A): The full $300,000 is not creditable; Section 960(d) applies a 20% haircut, limiting the credit to $240,000. Wrong (B): Section 78 gross-up applies to all deemed-paid credits, including those related to GILTI under Section 960. Wrong (C): The haircut is 80% (not 50%); it does not match the Section 250 deduction percentage.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — FATCA reporting requirements (Form 8938)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Harrison, a U.S. citizen living domestically, holds a foreign bank account with a maximum balance of $75,000 and foreign securities valued at $30,000 during the tax year. The total of Harrison's specified foreign financial assets is $105,000. Under FATCA (Section 6038D), must Harrison file Form 8938 with his tax return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because the FATCA threshold for domestic filers is $200,000 at year-end or $300,000 at any time during the year", "Yes, because the aggregate value exceeds the $50,000 year-end threshold or $75,000 at-any-time threshold for domestic individual filers", "No, because FATCA only applies to U.S. persons living abroad", "Yes, but only the foreign bank account must be reported, not the securities"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 6038D, unmarried domestic filers must file Form 8938 if specified foreign financial assets exceed $50,000 on the last day of the tax year or $75,000 at any time during the year. Harrison's $105,000 aggregate value exceeds both thresholds. Wrong (A): The $200,000/$300,000 thresholds apply to taxpayers living abroad, not domestic filers. Wrong (C): FATCA Form 8938 applies to all U.S. persons, including those living domestically, with lower thresholds than those living abroad. Wrong (D): Both foreign bank accounts and foreign securities are specified foreign financial assets that must be reported on Form 8938.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L3 — Subpart F same-country exception
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Archer Corp's CFC in Country R purchases components from an Archer-related manufacturer also in Country R and sells finished goods to customers in Country S. The CFC earns $4,000,000 from these sales. Archer's tax advisor argues the income is exempt from Subpart F under the same-country exception for foreign base company sales income. Under Section 954(d)(2), evaluate whether the exception applies.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The exception applies because both the CFC and the related manufacturer are incorporated in Country R, exempting the $4,000,000", "The exception does not apply because the goods are sold to customers outside Country R (in Country S), and the same-country exception requires the property to be sold for use within the CFC's country", "The exception applies because the CFC performs manufacturing activities that add substantial value", "The exception does not apply because same-country exception only exempts services income, not sales income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 954(d)(2), the same-country exception for foreign base company sales income exempts income from the purchase and sale of property when both the CFC and the related party from whom it purchases (or to whom it sells) are organized in the same country. Since both the CFC and the related manufacturer are in Country R, the purchase side of the transaction satisfies the same-country exception regardless of where the goods are ultimately sold. Wrong (B): The same-country exception looks at the relationship between the CFC and the related party, not the end customer's location. Wrong (C): The manufacturing exception under Section 954(d)(1) is a separate exclusion from the same-country exception. Wrong (D): The same-country exception under Section 954(d)(2) specifically applies to foreign base company sales income.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — FTC basket assignment for GILTI
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Sterling Corp has foreign-source income in three categories: $500,000 of passive investment income, $2,000,000 of general category active business income, and $1,500,000 of GILTI inclusion. Sterling pays foreign taxes of $100,000 on the passive income, $400,000 on the general income, and $200,000 in deemed-paid taxes on GILTI. Into which FTC basket does the GILTI inclusion fall?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The passive category basket because GILTI is derived from CFC earnings", "The general category basket because GILTI is active intangible income", "A separate GILTI basket (Section 904(d)(1)(A)) distinct from both general and passive categories", "GILTI has no basket assignment because deemed-paid credits bypass the limitation system"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 904(d)(1)(A), GILTI is assigned to its own separate foreign tax credit basket. This prevents GILTI credits from cross-subsidizing or being offset by credits in the general or passive baskets, ensuring the GILTI limitation is computed independently. Wrong (A): GILTI is not placed in the passive basket despite some CFC income being passive in nature. Wrong (B): GILTI has its own dedicated basket rather than being pooled with general category income. Wrong (D): Deemed-paid credits on GILTI are subject to the FTC limitation computed within the separate GILTI basket.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Transfer pricing arm's length standard definition
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under Section 482, what standard does the IRS apply when evaluating transfer prices between related parties?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The cost recovery standard, requiring prices to cover manufacturing costs only", "The fair market value standard used in estate and gift taxation", "The arm's length standard, requiring prices consistent with those between unrelated parties dealing at arm's length", "The book value standard, requiring prices to match GAAP-reported asset values"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 482 authorizes the IRS to reallocate income among related entities to clearly reflect income using the arm's length standard. This requires that related-party transactions produce results consistent with those between unrelated parties acting independently. Wrong (A): The cost recovery standard is not the standard under Section 482; prices must reflect arm's length results, not merely cover costs. Wrong (B): Fair market value is used in estate and gift tax contexts; Section 482 uses the arm's length standard. Wrong (D): Book value under GAAP is not the standard for transfer pricing purposes.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Section 962 election analysis for individual CFC shareholders
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Morgan, an individual U.S. shareholder, owns 100% of a CFC with $3,000,000 of GILTI tested income, $10,000,000 of QBAI, and $400,000 of foreign taxes paid on the tested income. Without a Section 962 election, Morgan's GILTI inclusion of $2,000,000 ($3,000,000 - 10% x $10,000,000) is taxed at Morgan's individual rate of 37%. With a Section 962 election, Morgan is taxed at corporate rates and may claim the Section 250 deduction and deemed-paid credits. Evaluate whether the Section 962 election benefits Morgan.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No benefit, because the Section 962 election increases Morgan's total tax to $740,000", "The election reduces Morgan's GILTI tax from $740,000 to approximately $164,000 by providing the Section 250 deduction and deemed-paid FTCs at corporate rates", "The election eliminates all U.S. tax on the GILTI inclusion because deemed-paid credits fully offset the corporate-rate tax", "The election only benefits shareholders with GILTI inclusions below $1,000,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Without the election, Morgan pays $2,000,000 x 37% = $740,000. With the Section 962 election, Morgan is taxed as a corporation: $2,000,000 GILTI plus Section 78 gross-up of $320,000 (80% x $400,000) = $2,320,000. Section 250 deduction of 50% = $1,160,000. Taxable = $1,160,000 x 21% = $243,600. Deemed-paid credit of $320,000 exceeds tax, so effective tax is approximately $0 on the GILTI portion (with excess credits lost). The election provides substantial savings versus $740,000. Wrong (A): The election dramatically reduces the tax; it does not increase it. Wrong (C): While the credits may exceed the corporate-rate tax on GILTI, actual distributions later may face additional individual-level tax. Wrong (D): The election benefits any individual shareholder facing rates above the effective corporate GILTI rate, regardless of the inclusion amount.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Section 245A participation exemption overview
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under Section 245A, what percentage deduction does a domestic C corporation receive for the foreign-source portion of dividends from a specified 10%-owned foreign corporation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["100% dividends received deduction", "50% dividends received deduction", "70% dividends received deduction", "80% dividends received deduction"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 245A provides a 100% dividends received deduction for the foreign-source portion of dividends received from specified 10%-owned foreign corporations by domestic C corporations. This participation exemption system, enacted by the TCJA, moves the U.S. toward a territorial tax system for active foreign earnings. Wrong (B): The 50% deduction applies to GILTI under Section 250, not to Section 245A dividends. Wrong (C): The 70% DRD applies to dividends from domestic corporations under Section 243(a)(1) when ownership is below 20%. Wrong (D): There is no 80% DRD under Section 245A; the deduction is 100%.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Foreign currency transaction gain/loss
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under Section 988, how are gains and losses from foreign currency transactions generally characterized for U.S. tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["As capital gains and losses", "As ordinary income or loss", "As tax-exempt income", "As passive activity income or loss"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 988 provides that gains and losses arising from changes in exchange rates on certain foreign currency transactions (including debt instruments, receivables, payables, and forward contracts) are generally treated as ordinary income or loss. This ensures currency fluctuations are treated as part of normal business operations. Wrong (A): Section 988 overrides the general capital gain/loss rules; foreign currency transaction gains and losses are ordinary, not capital. Wrong (C): Foreign currency gains are taxable income; they are not exempt. Wrong (D): Section 988 gains and losses are characterized as ordinary, not as passive activity income; passive activity classification depends on the underlying activity.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
